F863 3706             (    forth6809.asm):18904         UNESCAPEW: PULU D        ; BUG FIX: was PULU D/STD UESRCLEN (storing
F865 DDAB             (    forth6809.asm):18905                    STD  UEDST     ; the popped dest-addr into the SOURCE-
                      (    forth6809.asm):18906                                   ; length variable), then LDD ,U (a PEEK, not
                      (    forth6809.asm):18907                                   ; a pop) into BOTH UEADDR and UEDST - so the
                      (    forth6809.asm):18908                                   ; real source length was misread as an
                      (    forth6809.asm):18909                                   ; address, the real source address was never
                      (    forth6809.asm):18910                                   ; popped at all, and the true destination
                      (    forth6809.asm):18911                                   ; address never reached UEDST. Correct
                      (    forth6809.asm):18912                                   ; ANS order is ( c-addr1 u1 c-addr2 -- ):
                      (    forth6809.asm):18913                                   ; c-addr2 (dest) is on top, popped first.
F867 3706             (    forth6809.asm):18914                    PULU D         ; next: u1 (source length)
F869 DDA9             (    forth6809.asm):18915                    STD  UESRCLEN
F86B 3706             (    forth6809.asm):18916                    PULU D         ; next: c-addr1 (source address)
F86D DDA7             (    forth6809.asm):18917                    STD  UEADDR
F86F CC0000           (    forth6809.asm):18918                    LDD  #0
F872 DDAD             (    forth6809.asm):18919                    STD  UEOUTLEN
F874 9EA7             (    forth6809.asm):18920                    LDX  UEADDR
F876 109EAB           (    forth6809.asm):18921                    LDY  UEDST
                      (    forth6809.asm):18922         
                      (    forth6809.asm):18923                    ; ALGORITHM REPLACED (caught by MAME testing after the
                      (    forth6809.asm):18924                    ; argument-order fix above): the entire body below used
                      (    forth6809.asm):18925                    ; to decode backslash escape sequences (\n, \t, \\, \") -
                      (    forth6809.asm):18926                    ; a plausible-looking but entirely wrong reading of what
                      (    forth6809.asm):18927                    ; ANS UNESCAPE does. Confirmed against the actual spec and
                      (    forth6809.asm):18928                    ; its canonical reference implementation (forth-standard.
                      (    forth6809.asm):18929                    ; org/standard/string/UNESCAPE and complang.tuwien.ac.at's
                      (    forth6809.asm):18930                    ; ANS Forth reference text): UNESCAPE has nothing to do
                      (    forth6809.asm):18931                    ; with backslash sequences at all - it replaces every '%'
                      (    forth6809.asm):18932                    ; character with two '%' characters, and nothing else, so
                      (    forth6809.asm):18933                    ; that a literal '%' in text survives an eventual
                      (    forth6809.asm):18934                    ; SUBSTITUTE pass unchanged ("If you pass a string through
                      (    forth6809.asm):18935                    ; UNESCAPE and then SUBSTITUTE, you get the original
                      (    forth6809.asm):18936                    ; string"). Every character that ISN'T '%' passes straight
                      (    forth6809.asm):18937                    ; through, one-for-one. Confirmed via MAME: doubling a
                      (    forth6809.asm):18938                    ; single '%' wasn't happening at all, and the returned
                      (    forth6809.asm):18939                    ; count didn't grow to reflect the added characters -
                      (    forth6809.asm):18940                    ; both are direct, expected consequences of this being the
                      (    forth6809.asm):18941                    ; wrong algorithm entirely, not a smaller bug within it.
